00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:20.279
On Thursday, May 3rd, 1979, 31,221,362 voters
gathered at polling stations across the United

00:00:20.279 --> 00:00:27.279
Kingdom to decide on who would fill the 650
seats of the House of Commons. Now, any election

00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:33.050
is important, but this one held particular
significance. Over the years leading up to

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:37.860
it, the country’s manufacturing industry
was collapsing, inflation was reeking havoc

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:42.680
on the economy, mass strikes were plaguing
both the public and private sector, unemployment

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:48.429
was exploding, and it was quickly becoming
clear that Britain was losing ground to Japan,

00:00:48.429 --> 00:00:53.269
Germany, the US, and other subjects of post-war
economic booms.

00:00:53.269 --> 00:00:57.960
Margaret Thatcher and her Conservative Party
proposed a solution: they would curb the power

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:04.221
of unions, cut personal income tax, and privatize
nationalized industries—essentially, it

00:01:04.221 --> 00:01:10.540
was a complete rejection of the economic ideology
of the prior 30 years. However, in the view

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:16.000
of the British public, the status quo was
what got them to the turmoil of the era, so

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.869
Thatcher’s message resonated. Once the ballots
were counted, the Conservative Party picked

00:01:20.869 --> 00:01:28.649
up 62 new seats, bringing their total to 339—a
majority—and therefore making Margaret Thatcher

00:01:28.649 --> 00:01:34.910
Prime Minister of the United Kingdom.
The era of British privatization started quite

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:39.060
quickly: first with British Petroleum, of
which Thatcher began started selling off shares

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:43.759
just months following her appointment. But
then, in the new decade, the trend kicked

00:01:43.759 --> 00:01:49.820
into high gear with British Aerospace, British
Gas, Rolls-Royce, British Airways, British

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:55.700
Steel, National Express, British Telecom,
British Leyland, the British Airports Authority,

00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:01.189
and dozens more state-owned enterprises being
sold off to the private sector. By the 1990s,

00:02:01.189 --> 00:02:06.570
the concept of a “state-owned enterprise”
was becoming increasingly obscure, but there

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:13.410
was just one major sector that the sells-offs
had yet to really touch: the railroads.

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:20.690
Thatcher, herself, didn't dare touch them.
Politically, it was tricky: in the country,

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:26.840
trains operated at a loss to many rural areas
from which her party needed votes. Fully privatizing

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:31.069
the industry would mean that those areas would
inevitably lose service, and the Conservative

00:02:31.069 --> 00:02:37.400
Party would inevitably lose votes. In addition,
in her view, railroads were one of the rare

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:43.069
examples of sectors that just had to be publicly
run—they were just too tricky to run both

00:02:43.069 --> 00:02:49.140
efficiently and effectively with a purely
profit-seeking motive. Her reign ended, though,

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:54.430
in 1990, after a leadership battle within
her own party, and her successors weren’t

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:59.510
so cautious.
Conveniently, in 1991, the European Union

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:06.560
acted first. It issued directive 91/440, which
read, “whereas the future development and

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:10.970
efficient operation of the railway system
may be made easier if a distinction is made

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:15.489
between the provision of transport services
and the operation of infrastructure; whereas

00:03:15.489 --> 00:03:19.909
given this situation, it is necessary for
these two activities to be separately managed

00:03:19.909 --> 00:03:25.799
and have separate accounts.” Essentially,
what this said was that the companies, organizations,

00:03:25.799 --> 00:03:30.910
or agencies that actually operated the trains
had to be legally separate entities from those

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:36.110
that operated and owned the tracks upon which
trains operated. The reasoning stemmed from

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:41.610
the EU's wish for private rail competition
against the government run systems. They were

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:46.110
opening the door for companies to operate
rail services on any track in the European

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:52.069
Union, as long as they paid for it.
Of course, that gave the government a perfect

00:03:52.069 --> 00:04:00.209
first target for privatization. On April 1st,
1994, Railtrack came into existence—a private

00:04:00.209 --> 00:04:05.010
company that now owned all of the tracks,
stations, and rail infrastructure in the United

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:10.299
Kingdom. There was much work left to do, though:
British Rail’s trains went to three newly-formed

00:04:10.299 --> 00:04:16.079
rolling stock companies, their telecoms infrastructure
was sold to Racal Electronics, freight operations

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:21.350
were split up and allocated to six newly-formed
private companies, and this process went on

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:27.580
and on and on, but then came the question
of passenger train services—it was very

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:34.670
clear that the solution to privatizing those
would not be a quick and complete sell-off.

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:39.970
The crux of the problem was exactly what Thatcher
had identified when she decided not to privatize

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:46.840
the railways: the public had come to expect
and rely on inherently loss-making rail operations.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:51.730
While running an 8:00 am train from Edinburgh
to London may be profitable, running a 1:00

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:57.980
pm train from Manchester to Buxton probably
is not. In order to run trains at the prices,

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:04.270
to the places, and with the frequency the
public demanded, someone had to at least sometimes

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:10.100
take a loss.
With that, a structure evolved. InterCity,

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:14.980
British Rail’s long-distance brand, was
split into seven segments; Network SouthEast,

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:19.600
which primarily operated commuter services
in and around London, was split into ten;

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:24.220
while Regional Railways, which operated short-distance
services throughout the rest of the country,

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:29.500
was split into eight. These twenty-five Train
Operating Companies would at first be owned

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:34.420
and run by the government, but through time,
the operations of each would be franchised

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:39.960
out to private companies. Now, each of the
Train Operating Companies were designed so

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:45.030
that their advantages and disadvantages were,
in the view of a private company, somewhat

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:50.520
balanced. That’s rather important since
any franchisee is contractually required to

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:56.400
serve certain stations at certain times and
certain frequencies. For example, the InterCity

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.770
East Coast franchise includes the super high-demand,
high-frequency east-coast service from Edinburgh

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:06.861
Waverley to London Kings Cross—two of the
busiest train stations in the UK, between

00:06:06.861 --> 00:06:12.790
which any operator should be able to turn
a profit. However, this franchise also requires,

00:06:12.790 --> 00:06:17.460
for example, the operator to run at least
one train a day that leaves London Kings Cross

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:23.840
between 11:30 am and 12:30 pm, and eventually
calls at the small Scottish town of Kingussie,

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:27.960
along with a corresponding southbound service
that calls at the town before arriving at

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:35.140
Kings Cross between 5:30 and 6:30 pm. Now,
only some 108 people use Kingussie Railway

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:40.310
Station each day—most to get on the regional
trains running between Glasgow or Edinburgh

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:44.750
and Inverness. A private company likely would
not choose to service a station where they’d

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:49.290
be lucky to pick up a dozen passengers in
a day, so the intention of the franchising

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:54.950
system was to balance things out—put a little
bit of the bad with the good—so that places

00:06:54.950 --> 00:07:00.910
like Kingussie would not lose their service.
To further balance things out, the government

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:04.890
would accept bids from companies to operate
the different franchises, and what the government

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:10.350
would receive could either be in the positive
or the negative. The government would look

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:14.880
at all the serious bids and quite simply pick
the one that would make them the most money,

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:20.111
in incoming franchise payments, or lose them
the least money, in outgoing subsidies. On

00:07:20.111 --> 00:07:25.990
the surface, it seemed like the perfect system—the
free market would force companies to compete

00:07:25.990 --> 00:07:31.750
to win a franchise and, in the end, the government,
and by extension the British taxpayer, would

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:37.890
spend the least amount or earn the most amount
of money possible… but that’s just the

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:42.670
view from the surface.
If you dive in and take a deeper look at the

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:47.310
franchise agreement, the contract between
an operator and the government, cracks begin

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:53.500
to show. This document is the draft of that
agreement for the InterCity East Coast Franchise—the

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:57.530
one that includes Edinburgh to London Service—
published by the UK government during the

00:07:57.530 --> 00:08:03.180
latest tendering process in 2014. It begins
with definitions of terms, a statement of

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:08.630
governing law, and other assorted legal declarations,
but then moves on to exactly how the relationship

00:08:08.630 --> 00:08:14.650
between a franchisee and the government will
work. For example, in Schedule 1.1, Part A,

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:18.980
Section 4, it lays out the idea that franchisees
cannot alter the timetable without a long,

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:23.560
arduous process of consultation with the government
and other stakeholders—including the public.

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:27.380
Section 7 established the requirements for
operators to, on average, have the capacity

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:31.200
for anyone to find a seat immediately upon
boarding on off-peak trains, and within 20

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:35.840
minutes of boarding for on-peak trains. Section
12 introduces the concept of breach performance

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:40.520
levels—which, if a franchisee exceeds, will
put them in breach of contract. For example,

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:46.390
the InterCity East Coast franchisee must not
exceed 0.0235 cancellations per 1,000 miles

00:08:46.390 --> 00:08:50.750
or 1,600 kilometers travelled in its second
year of operations, and by the end of year

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:56.260
10, if their franchise even lasts that long,
the level must be down to 0.0119. Schedule

00:08:56.260 --> 00:09:00.450
1.2, Part A, Section 6 lays out the requirements
for alternate transportation in the event

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:04.220
of train cancellation; this section outlines
the requirements for timetable publishing

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:09.140
and poster display at stations; this one defines
the operator’s data reporting requirements;

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:12.760
this outlines the requirement for operators
to accept folding bikes on all its services,

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:17.080
and full-size bikes “wherever reasonably
practical”; this establishes the prohibition

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:21.030
of the franchisees engaging in any business
except for the operation of trains and certain

00:09:21.030 --> 00:09:23.320
ancillary services like selling food onboard;
this lays out the train fleet for which the

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:25.630
franchisee must take over the leases; this
outlines the franchisee’s ban on entering

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:29.830
into any leases without government approval,
and the leases for stations, depots, and other

00:09:29.830 --> 00:09:34.051
facilities that it’s required to enter into;
and this section summarizes what that franchisee

00:09:34.051 --> 00:09:38.920
is legally required to help with for the operation
of the Queen or subsequent monarch’s train.

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:44.280
So, the government tells franchisees where
they must operate, when they must operate,

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:49.280
which trains they must operate, which facilities
they must lease, the maximum amount they can

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:55.140
charge, how long they can operate: so the
question is, with very, very little ability

00:09:55.140 --> 00:10:01.140
to choose how to run their trains, where are
they supposed to compete?

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:07.610
The answer is the bidding process. That’s
essentially the only step in this process

00:10:07.610 --> 00:10:12.960
where free-market competitive economics come
into play, but these forces pushed bidders

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:19.210
to over-promise and under-deliver. They’d
say they expected a certain number of passengers,

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:23.430
translating to a certain amount of profit,
leading to a certain hypothetical future franchise

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:28.500
payment: the main number that the government
looks at in a bid. If those passengers didn't

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:33.480
appear as predicted, then the profit didn’t
come either, and the franchisee would fail

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:40.210
to make the payments upon which it bid. This
is a cycle that happened time and time and

00:10:40.210 --> 00:10:46.530
time again. GNER, owned by Sea Containers,
was the first operator of the InterCity East

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:51.110
Coast Franchise, before it overbid in its
renewal, failed to make its payments to the

00:10:51.110 --> 00:10:56.180
government, and lost the franchise early.
To replace them, the government awarded the

00:10:56.180 --> 00:11:01.090
franchise to National Express in 2007, who
would operate the services under the National

00:11:01.090 --> 00:11:07.130
Express East Coast brand. However, just two
years later, it emerged that they too had

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:11.840
forecast more passengers than emerged in reality,
were not able to make the payments they had

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:18.710
bid on, and so they lost the franchise in
2009. After this experience, the government

00:11:18.710 --> 00:11:23.920
temporarily re-nationalized the railway under
the “operator of last resort” scheme that

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:29.340
had been set up for this exact situation.
The succinctly named “East Coast” company,

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:35.060
owned and run by the government, operated
the line until a new bidding process was opened

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:39.220
in 2013. A joint venture between Stagecoach
and the Virgin Group won the bid, and operated

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:45.440
services under the Virgin Trains East Coast
Brand. It turned out that passenger, and therefore

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:50.090
revenue, growth had not matched the levels
they based their bid upon, therefore they

00:11:50.090 --> 00:11:54.510
were hemorrhaging money, therefore they could
not make their franchise payments to the government,

00:11:54.510 --> 00:12:00.130
therefore they lost the franchise in 2018,
and once again, the government re-nationalized

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:05.940
the line and operated services under the London
North Eastern Railway Brand.

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:11.811
Throughout this nearly 25-year cycle, only
one period stood out from the rest. Between

00:12:11.811 --> 00:12:18.560
November 14th, 2009 and February 28th, 2015,
when the line was run by the government owned

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:25.270
“East Coast” train operating company,
things went… well. In its final full year

00:12:25.270 --> 00:12:30.490
of operation, it was tied for fourth among
23 train operating companies in terms of overall

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:36.340
passenger satisfaction, fifth for punctuality,
fourth for value for money, and, even while

00:12:36.340 --> 00:12:41.710
being one of the most popular train companies,
it generated over £1 billion in profit over

00:12:41.710 --> 00:12:47.610
the years that went straight back to the government
and, by extension, the British public. Therefore,

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:55.570
when it was announced that the franchise was
to be reprivatized, many asked why? Why should

00:12:55.570 --> 00:13:01.490
the government hand over a well-run, well-liked,
well-profitable company to private hands which

00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:06.530
will, at best, just take a portion of those
profits away from the British government and

00:13:06.530 --> 00:13:10.180
people?
Over the following years, this and other related

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:15.810
questions, doubting the merits of this almost
entirely unique franchise model, grew louder

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:21.920
and louder. By the start of 2020, an increasing
number of railways stopped operating under

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:26.710
the franchising model: instead, they were
run as concessions, where the government contracts

00:13:26.710 --> 00:13:30.830
a company to operate trains while taking the
financial risk themselves; open access operators,

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:34.810
where private companies simply pay for track
access, typically on the most lucrative routes,

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:38.860
without any additional agreement with the
government; or the operator of last resort

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:44.029
model, such as with the East Coast franchise,
where a line is renationalized and government-run.

00:13:44.029 --> 00:13:50.390
Simultaneously, it was getting tougher and
tougher to find new, reputable bidders for

00:13:50.390 --> 00:13:55.310
franchises up for renewal. Many were extended
through direct award, where the government

00:13:55.310 --> 00:14:01.339
skips the competitive bidding process, which
goes against the original vision of rail franchising.

00:14:01.339 --> 00:14:06.560
All around, the viability of the franchising
model was coming more and more into question

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:11.730
both domestically and internationally, where
complete rail privatization was something

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:16.580
few countries had even considered—let alone
implemented.

00:14:16.580 --> 00:14:24.510
Like with so many things, though, March, 2020
dealt a death blow to the United Kingdom’s

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:30.070
privatized railroads. With the onset of stay-at-home
orders in Britain, passenger numbers fell

00:14:30.070 --> 00:14:36.460
through the floor, and it became very clear,
very quickly that no franchisee would be able

00:14:36.460 --> 00:14:43.730
to fulfill their obligations to the government.
Therefore, after years, even decades of debate

00:14:43.730 --> 00:14:50.550
on whether the railroads should be renationalized,
it just happened… in a day. Secretary of

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:55.640
State for Transport Grant Schnapps issued
a letter to Parliament, stating that all franchisees

00:14:55.640 --> 00:15:00.770
would immediately be shifted to a concession
model. The government would pay all their

00:15:00.770 --> 00:15:06.190
costs, plus a small management fee equal to
no more than 2% of the company’s costs in

00:15:06.190 --> 00:15:11.490
the year leading up to the pandemic. Under
any definition, even if private companies

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:18.589
were operating the trains, this was a nationalized
railway. Originally, this was to go on only

00:15:18.589 --> 00:15:24.560
for six months, just as a temporary measure,
but in the final days of that period, on September

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:33.860
21st, another announcement was made: after
24 years, rail franchising, as an operating

00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:40.060
model, was done… forever.
Now, as of yet, it’s not yet known exactly

00:15:40.060 --> 00:15:45.700
how the country’s railways will operate
once the provisional recovery measures lapse.

00:15:45.700 --> 00:15:50.450
The most we know comes from the Transport
Secretary, who said in the initial announcement,

00:15:50.450 --> 00:15:55.740
“The model of privatisation adopted 25 years
ago has seen significant rises in passenger

00:15:55.740 --> 00:16:00.839
numbers, but this pandemic has proven that
it is no longer working.

00:16:00.839 --> 00:16:06.800
Our new deal for rail demands more for passengers.
It will simplify people’s journeys, ending

00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:11.180
the uncertainty and confusion about whether
you are using the right ticket or the right

00:16:11.180 --> 00:16:16.040
train company. It will keep the best elements
of the private sector, including competition

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:21.180
and investment, that have helped to drive
growth, but deliver strategic direction, leadership

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:27.870
and accountability. Passengers will have reliable,
safe services on a network totally built around

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:36.360
them. It is time to get Britain back on track.”
Now, there has always been and will always

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:41.800
be debate on whether the privatization of
the UK’s railways was a failure. It brought

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:46.251
the highest number of passengers onto the
rails in the country's history—higher even

00:16:46.251 --> 00:16:51.170
than during the golden age of rail transport
in the early 1900s—but it also brought,

00:16:51.170 --> 00:16:56.230
by most measures, some of the highest fares
in all of Europe. It increased the public’s

00:16:56.230 --> 00:17:00.770
satisfaction in the railroads and brought
it to one of the highest levels in Europe,

00:17:00.770 --> 00:17:04.870
but, counterintuitively, it also pulled the
government’s rail subsidy up to its highest

00:17:04.870 --> 00:17:10.970
level in history. Quite decisively, after
privatization, the decline of the UK's rail

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:15.959
industry turned around for the better, but
what's not clear is how much of that can be

00:17:15.959 --> 00:17:21.020
attributed to the privatization itself—rather
than the increased focus that privatization

00:17:21.020 --> 00:17:26.660
brought to the rails. What is clear, though,
is that the experiment, itself, failed in

00:17:26.660 --> 00:17:31.870
proving the resilience of the franchising
model. Despite positive public sentiment about

00:17:31.870 --> 00:17:38.250
the railways as a whole, an overwhelming majority
of the British public supported renationalization.

00:17:38.250 --> 00:17:43.810
Therefore, it did not start a domino effect
of rail privatization across Europe and the

00:17:43.810 --> 00:17:49.480
world, it did not convince the general public
in its merits, and it proved, most of all,

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:54.620
that the model was fundamentally flawed as
it only worked in the best of times, and the

00:17:54.620 --> 00:18:00.440
worst of times will always eventually come.
While the privatization and deregulation of

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:05.800
the air transport industry, for example, rail's
most direct competitor, is almost universally

00:18:05.800 --> 00:18:10.530
viewed as a success that brought lower fares
and industry growth to the point that state-owned

00:18:10.530 --> 00:18:16.880
airlines are now largely a relic of antiquity,
the same cannot be said for rail. No country

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:23.520
has really cracked the nut of fully privatized
passenger train service in the 21st century.

00:18:23.520 --> 00:18:28.620
While many have taken half-measures such as
privatizing freight, the rails, awarding concessions

00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:33.260
to private operators but taking on the financial
risk like the UK now does, or even doing as

00:18:33.260 --> 00:18:38.380
the UK did before and effectively fully privatizing
railways, none have landed on a privatization

00:18:38.380 --> 00:18:45.390
model successful enough for other countries
to replicate it en mass. So, even if the UK’s

00:18:45.390 --> 00:18:50.530
privatization experiment accomplished some
of its goals, it failed on the most elusive

00:18:50.530 --> 00:18:56.590
